# The three-star, played: the opening of Mo Li Hua, E E G A C C A G in C (the score in the Wikipedia
# article on the song), moved onto the black keys. The black keys are the pentatonic on F sharp, so C
# becomes F sharp and every note moves up six semitones: A sharp, C sharp, D sharp, F sharp. Repeated
# notes are heard as one by music.plays, so the check lists the six changes of note.
item music.penta-transpose v=1 stars=3 skills=[music.pentatonic, music.keys, reasoning.plan] {
  title "Jasmine Flower on the black keys"

  scene 34x30 {
    notes tune clef=treble notes=[E4, E4, G4, A4, C5, C5, A4, G4] values=[1, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 1] meter=0 at=canvas(1, 1)
    text ask "This is how Mo Li Hua begins, in C. Its notes are all in the pentatonic scale on C. Play the same tune using only black keys, starting on A sharp, so that it sounds the same, only higher." narrate width=32 below=tune gap=1
    piano keys from=A4 whites=7 wide=3 labels=letters below=ask gap=1
  }

  check music.plays notes=[As4, Cs5, Ds5, Fs5, Ds5, Cs5]
  hint "The black keys make the pentatonic scale on F sharp. Which black key plays the part of C?"
  hint "E is the third note of the scale on C. Which is the third note of the black-key scale from F sharp? Match the others the same way."
}
